**Setting.** (M, g) a spacetime, causal relation ≤ (p ≤ q iff there is a future-directed causal curve from p to q, or p = q). J^-(q) = {p ∈ M : p ≤ q} (causal past, including q). gamma: [0,T) → M a future-directed causal geodesic (hence causal curve: t1 < t2 ⟹ gamma(t1) ≤ gamma(t2)).

**Predicate T(q, t0)** (tail): gamma([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M.
**Predicate W(q)** (whole): gamma([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M.

Since [0,T) ⊇ [t0,T), W(q) ⟹ T(q,t0) for any t0 ∈ [0,T). [wait: W says all of gamma([0,T)) in J^-(q); T says the subset tail is in J^-(q); so yes W ⟹ T for every t0]

Conversely, T(q,t0) ⟹ W(q): take t ∈ [0,T). If t ≥ t0, then gamma(t) ∈ tail ⊆ J^-(q). If t < t0, then gamma(t) ≤ gamma(t0) (causality of gamma, since t < t0) and gamma(t0) ∈ tail ⊆ J^-(q) so gamma(t0) ≤ q. Transitivity of ≤ gives gamma(t) ≤ q, so gamma(t) ∈ J^-(q). Hence W(q). ∎

So for causal curves, the two predicates are logically equivalent. The strictness claim is false **provided**:
1. The class predicate is over future-directed causal curves (not arbitrary curves).
2. J^-(q) is the causal past and ≤ is transitive (standard).
3. The "whole-curve" variant is over the same curve family.

Potential escape hatches that could rescue strictness:
